(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-prognosis-outcomes-quiz"]={"title":"Prognosis and Outcomes Quiz 1 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What does five-year survival measure?","choices":["Percent of patients alive five years from a point in their disease","Percent of patients cured after five years of treatment","Percent of a population dying of the disease in five years","Percent of patients whose disease returns within five years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the starting point is some point in the course of the disease, which is why where that point falls matters. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"For which kind of cohort does a survival curve estimate survival?","choices":["The actual cohort studied, exactly","Every patient with the disease worldwide","A single patient followed to the end","A hypothetical cohort rather than an actual one"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the curve is an estimate built from the interval probabilities, not the direct record of an actual group. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What happens to a censored patient in a Kaplan-Meier analysis?","choices":["Is counted as a death at that time","Leaves the number at risk without counting as a death","Is counted as alive until the end of the study","Is replaced by a newly enrolled patient"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: a censored patient is no longer followed, so the number at risk falls without a death being recorded. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What can bias do in a survival cohort study?","choices":["Only exaggerate differences that exist","Only remove chance variation","Falsely create or obscure real differences","Improve the precision of every estimate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: bias can make a difference appear that is not there or hide one that is. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A treated group has a hazard ratio of 0.4 compared with an untreated group. The treated group's risk is what proportion of the untreated group's?","choices":["40 percent","60 percent","250 percent","0.4 percent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: a hazard ratio below 1 is protective, and 0.4 means the treated group has 40 percent of the risk of the untreated group. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is zero time in a prognosis study?","choices":["The moment the last patient is enrolled","The date on which the study is published","A shared point in the disease course","The time at which the outcome occurs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: all cohort members should be identified at a similar point in the course of their disease. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which is a question to ask when appraising a prognosis study?","choices":["Were outcomes assessed objectively and blindly?","Was the exposure assigned by the investigator?","Were the participants free of the disease?","Was the study limited to a single center?"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the others are whether an inception cohort was assembled, whether entry criteria were objective, whether follow-up was adequate, whether the patients resemble yours and whether important prognostic factors were adjusted for. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Nine patients die after 3, 5, 6, 8, 11, 15, 20, 28 and 40 months. What is the median survival time?","choices":["15 months","11 months","8 months","20 months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the fifth of nine ordered values leaves four on each side, and the mean of 15.1 months is pulled up by the longest survivors. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What does a clinical prediction rule estimate?","choices":["The prevalence of disease in a community","Outcome probability from patient characteristics","The sensitivity of a test in a new setting","The best treatment from a list of drugs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, using a set of characteristics rather than one finding, which predicts outcome better than any single data point. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"As new treatments arise, what are they compared with?","choices":["The outcome in healthy people","The cost of the older treatment","The opinion of the treating clinician","The natural, untreated outcome of disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because the untreated course is the baseline that shows whether a new treatment changes what would otherwise happen. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Why is the generalizability of a prognosis study often limited?","choices":["Its follow-up is always too short","Its outcomes cannot be measured","Its patients may not represent others","Its patients were always randomized"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: they may also not represent an individual patient, so how similar they are to yours must be judged. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"How does the proportion of dropouts affect the potential for bias?","choices":["Potential for bias falls as dropouts increase","Potential for bias rises as dropouts increase","Dropouts have no effect on bias","Dropouts only matter when they recover"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the key question is whether dropouts are similar to those who stay in the study. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Why does the severity of a disease matter?","choices":["It sets priorities for health services","It fixes how many risk factors a patient has","It fixes the cost of every treatment","It removes the need to study outcomes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: knowing how severe the natural course is tells planners where clinical services and public health programs are most needed. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"The chances of surviving three successive intervals are 90, 80 and 50 percent. What is the probability of surviving all three?","choices":["36 percent","73 percent","50 percent","90 percent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the cumulative probability is the product, 0.90 times 0.80 times 0.50, which is 0.36. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Where on a survival curve is the estimate most precise?","choices":["Late on, at the right side","Early on, at the left side","Wherever the curve plateaus","Equally at every point along the curve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: more of the population remains under observation early on, so the estimate is based on a larger group. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is prognosis?","choices":["The identification of a disease from its findings","The chance a healthy person develops a disease","The cause of a disease in one patient","A prediction of the course of a disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is what a clinician tries to estimate once a diagnosis is made, in terms such as survival, complications or disability. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What is migration bias?","choices":["Groups differ in disease course at entry","One group has its outcomes detected more often","Patients drop out of the study or change groups","Patients are chosen for convenience"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the reasons include death, recovery and disability. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which stage comes first in the clinical course of disease?","choices":["Biologic onset","Signs and symptoms present","Medical care sought","Diagnosis given"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the sequence runs from biologic onset through evident pathology, signs and symptoms, care sought, diagnosis and treatment to outcome. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"When in the course of disease can screening find it?","choices":["Before signs and symptoms appear","Only after a diagnosis is given","Only once medical care is sought","After the outcome has occurred"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, during the preclinical phase, which is what allows screening to detect disease at an earlier point. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What characterizes the preclinical phase of a disease?","choices":["Symptoms are present but no diagnosis exists","Treatment has begun but symptoms remain","The outcome has already occurred","Disease is present but symptoms have not appeared"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and this is the window in which screening can find disease, before signs and symptoms bring the patient to care. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which statement about prognosis is true?","choices":["It is what occurs without any intervention","It is fixed at the moment of biologic onset","It cannot be expressed in numbers","It can often be improved by clinical intervention"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, whereas natural history is what occurs without intervention, which is the contrast that gives prognosis its clinical value. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Five-year survival is 54 percent in people with a disease and 90 percent expected in people of the same age without it. What is the relative survival?","choices":["36 percent","167 percent","72 percent","60 percent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: observed survival divided by expected survival is 0.54 divided by 0.90, which is 0.60. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why can five-year survival not evaluate a therapy introduced two years ago?","choices":["Newer therapies never improve survival","No one has yet been followed for five years","Five-year survival applies only to screening","The natural history has been changed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: patients treated with a therapy introduced within the past five years, or with a disease duration under five years, cannot be evaluated. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which factor appears both as a risk factor for infarction and as a poor prognostic factor after it?","choices":["Anterior infarction","Ventricular arrhythmia","Cigarette smoking","Hypotension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: smoking raises the chance of infarction and also worsens the outlook after one, as does increasing age. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which of these is one of the five Ds used to describe the outcomes of disease?","choices":["Dissatisfaction","Diagnosis","Deterioration","Dependency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the five Ds are death, disease, discomfort, disability and dissatisfaction. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"How can prognosis be expressed quantitatively?","choices":["As the number of risk factors present","As the sensitivity of the test used","As a rate, such as five-year survival","As the cost of the treatment given"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and other examples are the case-fatality, response, remission and recurrence rates, each stating how often an outcome occurs. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"With what should the outcome of a new treatment be compared?","choices":["The course in people without the disease","The natural, untreated course of the disease","The cost of the most expensive alternative","The course in patients who were cured"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because only against the untreated outcome can a treatment's effect on prognosis be judged. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is an advantage of median survival time?","choices":["It uses every death in the cohort","It can be found before all have died","It adjusts for the age of the patients","It requires no follow-up of patients"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: it is also less affected by extreme values, so a few very long or short survivors do not move it much. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Besides biologic outcomes, what should outcome measurement include?","choices":["Only the date of death","Only laboratory values","Clinical outcomes and quality of life","Only outcomes the patient can hide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the full range of manifestations should be covered, biological and clinical, together with quality of life. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What does survival analysis calculate when people enter at various times?","choices":["The prevalence at a single moment","The sensitivity of the outcome measure","The random error of each measurement","The person-years each person contributes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: people can be added at different points in their disease and each contributes the time they are followed. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"}]};
